A||||11||WisconsinGT||Rotors and pads...||||1||
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||03-01-2000||08:28 PM||||I need to update my brake system. At the moment I need Rotors and pads. Stock will do. Anyone know where I can get them for less then I would pay at the Fiero store? $50.00 per rotor, $40.00 for the pads. Thanks. <P>Also, I am looking for stock rims for my 88GT. They need to be black and in like new condition. If someone can point me in the right direction, I would be greatfull.||||reg||1
Z||000001||TOM||03-01-2000||09:54 PM||||I just sold some 88 rotors that were never cut for $10 each||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||03-02-2000||01:29 AM||||I would suggest the Carbomite brake pads from the Fiero store. They work GREAT. Much better than regluar semi-metallic. It's worth the extra $$. I bought the cross-drilled rotors and the Carbomite pads. They work much better than the stock parts.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Uaana||03-02-2000||02:59 AM||||Another alternative is the grand am/baretta<BR>upgrade.. if your rotors are shot they make nice donor hubs and you get better braking at the expense of your parking brake..<BR>As for the rims try kick hill their prices are pretty decent.. or call your local bone yard and have them do a search for you.<BR>If you want nice looking rims try getting a set that are in fair shape normally about 35-50 apiece and have them refurbished for about 30 apiece.. looks better than old rims and cheaper than buying A+ quality rims that you cant inspect.||||reg||1
Z||000004||WisconsinGT||03-02-2000||05:00 AM||||THanks everyone for your help. Yeah, I can go with crossdrilled. But I was told it is overkill for a normally driven 88GT. Plus, I thought that the Grand-am upgrade was for pre 88 Fiero's. No? <P>I have the stock rims now. But they need to be redone. The price I am going to pay to fix them is far greater then $30.00 apiece. More like $170.00 apiece.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||03-02-2000||07:42 AM||||Yeah, the Grand Am swap is for the pre-88 Fieros. The 88 models already have the improved brakes. Have you tried wrenchhead.com for the brake parts?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Shiner||03-02-2000||09:35 AM||||I belive that wrenchhead.com sells Raybestos brake pads.  Get the semi-metallics.  These are the only brake pads I use for my fieros.  With no offense to Oreif, pads with carbon usually don't work as well on the street as normal old semi's.  The more carbon there is in the brake pad, the more quickly it dissipates heat.  While this is great for the track, where you are on the brakes all the time, and on them HARD, the pads will stay too cool on the street, and not provide their maximum capabilities most of the time.  <P> Cross drilled rotors sure do look nice, but they are much more prone to cracking, usually along the holes.  Again, these are not going to hold the heat as well, which is optimal in a race track situation.  <P> I just did the rears on my '87 GT with raybestos, and had the rotors cut.  I have a set of front pads, but I think I am going to do the grand-am upgrade.  I need to keep my parking brake, so the rears are out of the question until somebody comes up with a solution.  <P>Just my 2.365 cents  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||03-02-2000||11:42 AM||||I just put the Carbomet pads and new rotors on my 88 Formula. The pedal is *much* harder than it was with the old pads. Locking the wheels is pretty much impossible. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||lawrence||03-02-2000||08:09 PM||||I have about 3K miles on the CarboMets on my 88GT.  I don't like them.  Would be willing to sell them at half price if you are interested.<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000009||Gokart||03-04-2000||06:14 AM||||lawrence, what don't you like about the pads? I'm going to need new ones sometime this summer, but can't afford cross-drilled rotors as well. Do these pads only improve braking with the c/d rotors? Is there another alternative for performance pads besides midas or autozone's?||||reg||5
Z||000010||batboy||03-04-2000||08:07 AM||||I was looking at brake upgrades last summer. I mentioned thinking about buying cross-drilled rotors too, but was told it doesn't do much for a typically driven street Fiero. I mistakenly thought the holes were drilled for cooling. Actually, the holes are mainly for allowing gases and debris (ground up brake pad, etc) to exit. This is usually only need under severe high speed and high performance driving like road racing. In some cases, the cross-drilling seems to weaken the rotor, if the pattern was not drilled perfect. In my opinion, save your money and upgrade to larger diameter vented rotors and calipers with bigger pistons.<P>The carbomet pads like to get nice and warm before they work great, so they will feel a little slippery during the first few stops on the way to work on a cold morning. Brake rotors do get hot after a few stops. I saw a video where they stuck a camera up under a NASCAR racer and sent it out onto the track for a few laps. Those rotors would instantly get red hot when the driver tapped the brakes. Premium name-brand semi-metallic pads are a good compromise, especially with the Grand Am brake swaps.||||reg||1
Z||000011||lawrence||03-04-2000||02:49 PM||||Gokart,<P>I don't have cross-drilled rotors.  Since I don't have any experience with them, I can't say if they are any better, but the people I talk to say that it doesn't help braking and really chews up the pads.  However, nobody has shown me any data either way.<P>What I don't like about the pads is that they are very hard, and DO take some time to warm up.  After my upgrade, they work pretty well, but I'm sure that I can get better INITIAL braking with standard pads.  I don't go down long inclines, and there aren't any roads around here that I would consider race course material, so I've never had any problem with fade.  What I AM interested in is when a kid runs out in front of the car, or when someone cuts me off.  That's when I need the most braking.<P>What I DO like about the pads is that they don't make any noise, and they don't make much dust.  I think I can find a set that are quiet, and I don't mind cleaning the dust since I wash the car once a week (need it or not  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">).<P>That's why I'd be willing to sell them.<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
